# ============================================================================
# MedDoc Pro — Caddy Reverse Proxy
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# T-M19 (2026-04-21): cut over from MODE A (plaintext, IP-based) to MODE B
# (HTTPS, domain-based). Live hostnames:
#
#   quillmd.com + www.quillmd.com  →  marketing  (port 3010 internal)
#   app.quillmd.com                →  TranscribeMD (port 3011 internal)
#   pro.quillmd.com                →  MedDoc Pro CRM (port 3000 internal)
#
# Caddy auto-provisions + auto-renews Let's Encrypt certs for each hostname
# (persisted in the `caddy_data` volume). HTTP→HTTPS redirect is automatic
# once a hostname is configured.
#
# The MODE A blocks are kept below as commented rollback reference. DO NOT
# uncomment them while MODE B is active: the MODE B HSTS header
# (max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains) tells every client that has hit us
# to refuse plaintext for up to a year. Safe-rollback procedure is:
#   1. Serve `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=0` over HTTPS first
#   2. Wait for clients to re-fetch and clear their cache
#   3. Then flip back to MODE A
# ============================================================================

# ─── Global options — trusted proxy chain (S2) ────────────────────────────
# Ingress is: internet → HOST nginx (owns :80/:443) → Caddy at 127.0.0.1:8443
# → app containers. From inside the container the nginx hop appears as the
# docker bridge gateway 172.19.0.1, so EVERY request looked like it came from
# 172.19.0.1 — all 2300 prod audit rows recorded that one address and per-IP
# login rate limiting was effectively global.
#
# Declaring the hop trusted is what makes {client_ip} resolve from the
# X-Forwarded-For header at all; without it, {client_ip} is just the immediate
# peer (the bridge gateway) and nothing changes.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING EITHER LAYER — the two halves are load-bearing
# TOGETHER, and this config is NOT self-sufficient:
#
#   Caddy's {client_ip} takes the LEFTMOST value of X-Forwarded-For when the
#   immediate peer is trusted. It does NOT walk the chain right-to-left looking
#   for the first untrusted hop. So on its own, trusting the hop would turn a
#   collapsed client IP into an ATTACKER-CHOSEN one: nginx used to append with
#   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for, so a request carrying "X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4"
#   arrived as "1.2.3.4, <realIP>" and the leftmost value won. This was measured,
#   not assumed — a probe with a forged header landed 1.2.3.4 in audit_logs.
#
#   The spoof resistance therefore lives in HOST nginx, which now REPLACES the
#   header with $remote_addr (see /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/quillmd). By the time
#   the chain reaches Caddy it holds exactly one trustworthy value.
#
# If nginx is ever reverted to $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for, this block silently
# becomes a client-IP spoofing vector.
{
    servers {
        trusted_proxies static 127.0.0.1/32 ::1 172.19.0.0/16
    }
}

# ─── Shared header snippet — MODE A (plaintext-safe) ──────────────────────
# Kept so the rollback commented block below still parses. No active block
# currently imports it.
(security_headers_mode_a) {
    header {
        X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
        X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
        Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        Permissions-Policy "microphone=(self), camera=(self), geolocation=()"
        -Server
    }
}

# ─── Shared header snippet — MODE B (HTTPS-only) ──────────────────────────
# Imported from every active domain block below. Adds HSTS + enforced CSP
# on top of the MODE A subset. Phase 19 (h16-csp-report-only): switched
# from Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only to enforced after the burn-in
# window from Phases 13-17 produced zero CSP violation reports.
# 'unsafe-inline' + 'unsafe-eval' are both required by Next 16 + Tailwind
# inline emission.
(security_headers_mode_b) {
    header {
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload"
        X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
        X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
        Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        Permissions-Policy "microphone=(self), camera=(self), geolocation=()"
        Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.ingest.sentry.io; frame-ancestors 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none';"
        -Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only
        -Server
    }
}

# ==========================================================================
# MODE B — active
# ==========================================================================

# ─── quillmd.com + www.quillmd.com — Marketing site ───────────────────────
# ─── WHY EVERY BLOCK BELOW CARRIES `tls internal` ─────────────────────────
# Caddy is NOT the public edge. Host nginx owns :80/:443 and terminates the
# real Let's Encrypt cert; Caddy is an INTERNAL hop on 127.0.0.1:8443 that
# nginx reaches with `proxy_ssl_verify off` (/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/quillmd).
# So the certificate on this hop is never seen by a browser and never needs to
# be publicly trusted — `tls internal` (Caddy's own CA) is the correct issuer.
#
# ⚠️ AND PUBLIC ACME CANNOT SUCCEED HERE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC.
# Both challenge types are structurally blocked by the topology:
#   tls-alpn-01 → nginx owns :443, so the ALPN handshake never reaches Caddy
#                 ("remote error: tls: no application protocol")
#   http-01     → nginx owns :80 and serves /.well-known/acme-challenge from
#                 /var/www/certbot, so Caddy's token 404s
# Caddy therefore retried forever, fell back to acme-STAGING, and renewed
# nothing.
#
# ⚠️ HOW THAT BECAME AN OUTAGE — 2026-08-07, ~6 hours, ALL public hostnames.
# The expired certs were still being served FROM CADDY'S IN-MEMORY CACHE, so
# nothing looked wrong (nginx does not verify them). certmagic's storage clean
# removed them from disk on 2026-08-06; the 08:31 reboot dropped the memory
# cache, and Caddy came back with NO certificate for quillmd.com,
# www.quillmd.com or app.quillmd.com. Every fresh handshake got
# `tlsv1 alert internal error` (alert 80) and nginx answered 502. Requests on
# already-resumed TLS sessions kept working, which is why containers were
# healthy and the failure looked intermittent.
#
# This fix is the follow-up filed at docs/qa-reports/site-down-20260603.md §9.5
# and never executed — recorded there as 🟢 "Caddy ACME noise". It was not
# noise: the certs it was failing to renew are the ones this hop serves.
# docs/DEPLOYMENT_RUNBOOK.md §5.6 has asserted "its own internal certs" since
# then; this is the line that makes that sentence true.
#
# DO NOT remove `tls internal` to "restore real certificates". Public certs on
# this hop are unobtainable while nginx owns the ports, and a browser never
# sees them. The public cert is nginx's, at
# /etc/letsencrypt/live/quillmd.com/fullchain.pem — renewed by certbot (§5.6).

# Apex + www both served from the same block so visitors land on the same
# content regardless of whether they type the `www.` prefix.
quillmd.com, www.quillmd.com {
    log {
        output stdout
        format console
    }

    tls internal

    import security_headers_mode_b

    reverse_proxy marketing:3010 {
        header_up Host {host}
        header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
    }
}

# ─── app.quillmd.com — TranscribeMD (pilot doctors log in here) ───────────
app.quillmd.com {
    log {
        output stdout
        format console
    }

    tls internal

    import security_headers_mode_b

    reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
        header_up Host {host}
        header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
    }
}

# ─── admin.quillmd.com — Platform admin dashboard (SUPER_ADMIN only) ──────
# Reverse-proxies to transcribe-md (which now owns the admin code, moved
# from meddoc-pro). The only path rewrite is `/` → `/admin` so the
# dashboard renders at the bare host root without forcing the user to
# type a path. Everything else (`/login`, `/api/auth/*`, `/api/admin/*`,
# `/_next/*`) passes through unchanged — those routes already work at
# their natural paths on the transcribe-md upstream.
#
# Cookie posture: host-only. A user logged in to app.quillmd.com (a
# clinician) does NOT auto-inherit a session here — admin requires its
# own sign-in. AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN stays unset to keep the cookie
# isolated per host. Security > UX for the admin surface.
#
# DNS: an A-record for admin.quillmd.com → this VPS must exist before
# Caddy can issue the Let's Encrypt cert (HTTP-01 validation). Until
# then this block still parses + Caddy serves on port 80 → 308 redirect
# to HTTPS, but the HTTPS handshake will use a fallback cert (curl -k
# tests work; browsers refuse).
admin.quillmd.com {
    log {
        output stdout
        format console
    }

    tls internal

    import security_headers_mode_b

    # ── Allowlist: only the routes a SUPER_ADMIN actually needs ──────
    # The first version of this block proxied EVERY path to the
    # transcribe-md upstream and only rewrote `/`. That meant a logged-
    # in clinician on admin.quillmd.com could navigate to /dashboard,
    # /patients, /visits, /settings, etc. and see the regular Transcribe-MD
    # UI on the admin host — wrong, and a privacy footgun. We now match
    # explicit allowlisted paths and 404 everything else.

    # NextAuth (csrf, session, callback) — pass through verbatim. This
    # MUST come before /api/admin/* because Caddy evaluates handle
    # matchers in declaration order; if /api/auth/* fell into the
    # /api/admin/* bucket it would 404.
    @auth path /api/auth/*
    handle @auth {
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Admin REST + SSE
    @adminApi path /api/admin/*
    handle @adminApi {
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Next.js asset bundles + static files (icons, fonts). Without
    # these the admin page would render unstyled.
    @nextStatic path /_next/* /favicon.ico /favicon.svg /robots.txt /manifest.webmanifest
    handle @nextStatic {
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Auth-flow pages a SUPER_ADMIN might genuinely use (sign-in,
    # password recovery). /signup is intentionally not allowed —
    # super-admins are provisioned out-of-band, never via self-service.
    @authPages path /login /forgot-password /reset-password /check-email
    handle @authPages {
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Admin pages — `/admin` itself + everything under it
    @adminPath path /admin /admin/*
    handle @adminPath {
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Root → /admin (the bare host serves the dashboard).
    @root path /
    handle @root {
        rewrite * /admin
        reverse_proxy transcribe-md:3011 {
            header_up Host {host}
            header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
            header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        }
    }

    # Anything else (e.g. /dashboard, /patients, /visits, /settings,
    # /signup) — 404 at the edge. Belt-and-braces alongside the strict
    # allowlist above; the catch-all guarantees that adding a new
    # transcribe-md route doesn't accidentally surface it on the
    # admin host.
    handle {
        respond "Not Found" 404
    }
}

# ─── pro.quillmd.com — MedDoc Pro CRM, TERMINATED (ruling #307) ─────────────
#
# ⚠️ THIS BLOCK USED TO PROXY TO A SERVICE THAT NO LONGER EXISTS.
#
# `meddoc-pro` was removed from `docker-compose.yml` (#307) and this block still
# read `reverse_proxy meddoc-pro:3000`. Nothing went red, because HOST nginx
# terminates `pro.quillmd.com` with an unconditional `return 410` and Caddy is
# never consulted for that hostname — so the dangling upstream was invisible.
#
# ⚠️ INVISIBLE IS NOT HARMLESS, AND THE ORDERING IS THE HAZARD.
# `DEPLOYMENT_RUNBOOK.md` §3.5's restore procedure deletes the nginx 410 block
# and reloads nginx BEFORE it touches the container. The moment that reload
# lands, this block is what answers — and with a dead upstream that is a **502
# wearing the retirement's place**, which `scripts/check-cotenants.sh` names as
# THE FINDING for this host.
#
# So the two layers now agree instead of one masking the other: nginx answers
# Gone at the edge, Caddy answers Gone behind it. Removing either control alone
# no longer produces a 502, and `check-cotenants.sh`'s "Caddy answers Gone" is
# true of the tree rather than merely believed.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS NOT A SECOND RETIREMENT DECISION — the owner ruling that took the
# host offline stands unchanged. It only removes the way this file could
# contradict it.
#
# RESTORING pro.quillmd.com NEEDS **THREE** REVERSALS, NOT TWO — the nginx 410
# block, this block, and the compose service. §3.5 carries the ordered
# procedure; the `@internal path` 404 that used to live here went with the
# upstream, because with no upstream there is no handler to hide.
pro.quillmd.com {
    log {
        output stdout
        format console
    }

    tls internal

    import security_headers_mode_b

    respond "MedDoc Pro was decommissioned on 2026-05-04 and taken offline on 2026-08-04." 410
}

